cription":"...The Flood Warning continues for the following rivers in Florida...\n\nSt Johns River Near Astor affecting Volusia and Lake Counties.\n\n.Persistent gusty northeast winds and upstream tidal effects are\nslowing river flow on the St Johns River at Astor. River levels are\nforecast to begin a slow decline late this week, but remain in\nModerate Flood stage for at least the next couple weeks.\n\nFor the St. Johns River...including Deland, Astor...Moderate flooding\nis forecast.\n\n* WHAT...Moderate flooding is occurring and is forecast.\n\n* WHERE...St Johns River near Astor.\n\n* WHEN...Until further notice.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 4.0 feet, Major flooding occurs with water entering\nmany structures on canals and along the river. Long duration\nflooding will occur, with levels taking weeks to months to return\nbelow flood stage. Water rescues will be necessary. River Road\nbecomes inaccessible, with 1 to 2 feet water depth in numerous\nhomes on Wildhog, Possum, Bonnet and Snail roads. Roads along Blue\nCreek are inaccessible with major flooding of homes on Otter,\nBranch and Chestnut roads. Camp South Moon Road and Juno Trail are\ninaccessible.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 8:30 AM EDT Wednesday the stage was 3.7 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 2.0 feet.\n- Recent Activity...The maximum river stage in the 24 hours ending\nat 8:30 AM EDT Wednesday was 3.8 feet.\n- Forecast...River levels are forecast to crest around 3.8 feet\nthis week, then begin a gradual decline late in the week.\n- Flood stage is 2.3 feet.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","instruction":"Motorists should not attempt to drive around barricades or drive cars\nthrough flooded areas.\n\nCaution is urged when walking near riverbanks.\n\nAdditional information is available at www.weather.gov.\n\nThe next statement will be issued late tonight at 200 AM EDT.","response":"Avoid","note":null,"parameters":{"AWIPSidentifier":["FLSMLB"],"WMOidentifier":["WGUS82 KMLB 281258"],"NWSheadline":["FLOOD WARNING REMAINS IN EFFECT UNTIL FURTHER NOTICE"],"BLOCKCHANNEL":["EAS","NWEM","CMAS"],"EAS-ORG":["WXR"],"VTEC":["/O.CON.KMLB.FL.W.0003.000000T0000Z-000000T0000Z/"],"expiredReferences":["w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.32a6624b03ca4bdc3923c468120fbeec7a6da027.002.1,2026-10-27T10:04:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.773baaf5fa5d14abca39f5240afdf228afff977d.001.1,2026-10-26T21:20:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6395ac22474085b0afc80efae484acd913f69a78.001.1,2026-10-26T09:55:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.216e8d1b705d46993d7c471c0b3a234344c7fb99.001.1,2026-10-25T21:25:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.15287a9048d637138112ab20fdb0596b03366bc8.001.1,2026-10-25T14:15:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.872c21e2071687af6bec24926666b78ae53500e9.001.1,2026-10-25T09:53:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4d219ec4da7afb70c15f06a098ffeafd908e065f.001.1,2026-10-24T20:58:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e654399ab6d3d3a1bec50bbcb60034d0ed66f1cf.001.1,2026-10-24T09:15:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8a86e36f7665671f8448c151681cfe66cfa60777.001.1,2026-10-23T21:27:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.773a119d76ff68fe774ba5c55884ee488b9b7674.001.1,2026-10-23T08:43:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.521f99544156044a319562a1dd43fdc776ad1874.001.1,2026-10-22T21:03:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4349c6b86ca16ae4a20ad3093a9f92ff4bf52ad2.001.1,2026-10-22T09:36:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.8dd3f7895f7084e9084bb38db419a134bf762f66.001.1,2026-10-21T21:21:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.22100c82ccb39acca4161f78436881e4db931fd8.001.1,2026-10-21T09:34:00-04:00 w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov,urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.39187c959e1ea92fa56dbf484ba09e62ca224e14.001.1,2026-10-21T09:31:00-04:00 w-